  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 4 Level 1 Walkthrough: Pharaoh's Tomb</h1>

    <p>Pharaoh's Tomb is the first room of Chapter 4, <i>Pharaoh's Bride</i>, and it is a different kind of room from the twenty-four that came before it in Chapter 3. Those were domestic spaces with one or two locks; this is a full Egyptian burial exhibit with nine separate puzzle and pickup stations, four of which are proper minigames. There is a sarcophagus on a plinth, a seated statue behind glass, blue and gold murals on every wall, and — unusually for this game — a restoration area at the back with a bed, a workbench and a stand for a clay model.</p>

    <p>Two things are worth knowing before you start. First, the room is a <b>hub</b>: most of its puzzles stand alone and can be worked in any order, so if one is annoying you, go do another. Second, the recording this page is built from stops one step short of the door — it ends with the game's own banner <b>This is the exit. Locked!</b> at 7:06. So this page maps the room honestly: every station, what it wants and what it gives, with no invented final code.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Read frame by frame at one frame per second from a <b>1080p capture of the full 7:06 run</b> (426 frames, no truncation). Each prop on this page was confirmed in at least <b>two separate frames</b> — for example the papyrus board at 0:25 and 0:28, the two-panel counter at 2:10 and 2:15, the arrow panel at 4:10, 5:00 and 5:20. Anything our two passes read differently is listed under “What this page could not verify” rather than printed as an answer.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={4} current={1} variant="jump" label="Chapter 4 · Pharaoh's Bride" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Pharaoh's Tomb is room 1 of 15 in Chapter 4, <i>Pharaoh's Bride</i>. Nine stations matter:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>0:16</b> — 4×4 sliding board of papyrus hieroglyph tiles, one empty slot.</li>
        <li><b>0:45</b> — take the ornate <b>gold key</b>.</li>
        <li><b>0:56–1:46</b> — <b>jigsaw</b>: rebuild the papyrus mural from polygonal shards.</li>
        <li><b>2:00–2:28</b> — <b>spot the difference</b> between two framed paintings, counter <b>0/10 → 10/10</b>.</li>
        <li><b>2:40</b> — the <b>four canopic jars</b> in the wall niche.</li>
        <li><b>4:04–5:35</b> — the <b>twelve-step arrow panel</b>; it pays out a <b>Clay sculpture</b>.</li>
        <li><b>5:55–6:10</b> — the glass <b>display case</b>: blue scarab, broad collar, and a <b>3×3 keypad</b>.</li>
        <li><b>6:35</b> — four wooden blocks whose cut-outs form a <b>face</b> (two eyes, one mouth).</li>
        <li><b>6:54</b> — pick up the <b>Anubis head</b>, then reach the door at 7:00 — still locked when the video ends.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="2SzJM3nlxkg" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 4 Level 1 Pharaoh's Tomb Walkthrough Pharaoh's Bride"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 4 Level 1 Pharaoh's Tomb">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/2SzJM3nlxkg/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 4 Level 1 Pharaoh's Tomb walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/2SzJM3nlxkg/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 7:06 Pharaoh's Tomb run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-hero-room.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 4 Level 1 Pharaoh's Tomb: a painted Egyptian burial chamber with a sarcophagus on a plinth, gold and blue murals, a seated statue and a work area" width="1200" height="675" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>One room, cut away like a stage set, with the camera free to pull in on any wall. The left half is the exhibit: a sarcophagus lying on a carved plinth, its lid painted with a face and a collar; a tall seated statue in a recess; and mural panels of musicians, boats and offering-bearers in the blue-and-ochre palette the chapter uses throughout. The right half is the working end of the room — a bed with red bedding against one wall, a bench loaded with pigment pots and brushes, a wooden stand holding whatever the arrow panel is building, and the glass display case that holds the level's centrepiece.</p>
    <p>The floor is plain, the walls are dense, and that ratio is the hint: <b>the walls are where the puzzles are</b>. Four of the ten interactive spots in this room are paintings or panels rather than furniture — the papyrus tile board, the two framed halves of the difference puzzle, the canopic niche and the arrow panel all sit flat against a wall.</p>
    <p>Structurally this is a hub, not a chain. Nothing in the room refuses to open because you have not finished something else; the order below simply follows the recording. The only real dependency we could see is that the clay sculpture only makes sense once you have seen the stand it belongs to.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Every station we could identify, in the order the run meets them.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Station</th><th>Where</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Papyrus tile board</td><td>Wall panel, left of the sarcophagus</td><td>4×4 grid of papyrus tiles printed with hieroglyphs (ankh, Eye of Horus, a woman's profile, a seated figure) and one empty slot — a sliding puzzle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gold key</td><td>Inventory pickup</td><td>Ornate gold key with a looped bow, labelled <b>Key</b> when it arrives.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shard jigsaw</td><td>Framed panel, centre wall</td><td>Polygonal shards that reassemble into a papyrus painting of Anubis, Horus and a pharaoh.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Two-panel difference board</td><td>Framed pair, right wall</td><td>The same banquet scene twice with a <b>0/10 → 10/10</b> counter: ten differences.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Canopic jars</td><td>Niche in the back wall</td><td>Four jars on a shelf, carved lids, hieroglyph bands.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arrow panel</td><td>Wall-mounted, five vertical tracks</td><td>Round stone buttons stamped with arrows, an artifact on a stand beside it, a counter to <b>12</b>. Reward: <b>Clay sculpture</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Display case</td><td>Glass case, right of the room</td><td>Winged blue scarab with a red sun disc, a blue-and-pale-green broad collar, and a <b>3×3 keypad</b> between them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Face blocks</td><td>Low track, front right</td><td>Four wooden blocks with rounded cut-outs that line up as two eyes and a mouth.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anubis head</td><td>Inventory pickup</td><td>Jackal-headed bust in dark stone with gold ear and collar detailing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exit door</td><td>Back wall, between two statues</td><td>Wooden door; the room labels it <b>This is the exit. Locked!</b> at the end of the run.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:16 · the first board</span>
      <strong>4×4 papyrus sliding tiles</strong>
      The first puzzle the run opens is a wall-mounted board of sixteen papyrus cells — fifteen tiles and one empty slot. The tiles carry Egyptian symbols rather than a photograph: in the frames we read, an <b>ankh</b>, the <b>Eye of Horus</b>, a woman's profile and a seated figure all appear, arranged so that identical symbols sit in pairs. One empty slot means this is a <b>sliding</b> puzzle, not a rotate-and-place one, so the pairs are the target and the empty cell is your working space. The run is in and out in under twenty seconds.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-papyrus-tiles.jpg" alt="The 4x4 papyrus tile board in Pharaoh's Tomb with hieroglyph tiles and one empty slot" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=16s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:16</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=28s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:28</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:45 · the pickup</span>
      <strong>The ornate gold key</strong>
      A gold key with a looped, ornamental bow arrives in the inventory at about <b>0:45</b> and the game labels it simply <b>Key</b>. It is the first of the room's two carried items. Keep it in mind for the display case and the door — the recording does not, in the end, spend it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-gold-key.jpg" alt="The ornate gold Key item in Pharaoh's Tomb, shown as an inventory pickup" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=45s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:45</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:56 to 1:46 · the long one</span>
      <strong>Rebuild the papyrus mural from shards</strong>
      The game pulls up a framed panel with white corner brackets and scatters <b>polygonal shards</b> across it — not the tab-and-blank jigsaw pieces you might expect, but flat angular fragments, each carrying a slice of the mural: the head of Anubis, the head of Horus, a pharaoh's gold face, pieces of hieroglyph border. Drag and rotate them until the painting is whole. At <b>1:12</b> the board is still empty of placed pieces, and the run works it until roughly <b>1:46</b> — it is the second-longest station in the room.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-jigsaw.jpg" alt="The shard jigsaw in Pharaoh's Tomb, polygonal fragments of a papyrus painting scattered inside a framed board" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=72s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:12</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=106s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:46</a></div>
    </div>

    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 2:00 to 2:28 · the ten</span>
      <strong>Two framed paintings, ten differences</strong>
      Two framed papyrus paintings open side by side — the same banquet scene, with a pharaoh in a white kilt on one side and a jackal-headed figure on the other — and a counter between them that starts at <b>0/10</b>. This is a spot-the-difference board: tap the element that differs and it is ringed on both copies. Our two reads at <b>2:10</b> and <b>2:15</b> show the counter at <b>2/10</b> and <b>5/10</b>, which confirms the count is ten differences and that the rings are progress, not errors. Work the borders first — the palm tree and the hieroglyph columns are where most of the differences sit in the frames we looked at.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-spot-difference.jpg" alt="The two-panel spot-the-difference board in Pharaoh's Tomb with a counter reading differences found out of ten" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=130s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:10</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=135s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:15</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 2:40 · the niche</span>
      <strong>Four canopic jars</strong>
      A wall niche opens on four canopic jars in a row, each with a carved lid and a band of hieroglyphs around the body. The run inspects them for about ten seconds and moves on. Nothing in the recording pairs the jars with a code, so read them as a clue source and part of the tomb's dressing rather than a lock to open.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-canopic-jars.jpg" alt="The four canopic jars in their wall niche in Pharaoh's Tomb, each with a carved lid and hieroglyph bands" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=160s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:40</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 4:04 to 5:35 · the twelve</span>
      <strong>The arrow panel that pays out a clay sculpture</strong>
      The room's longest puzzle sits on a wall panel split in two: on the left, <b>five vertical tracks</b> holding round stone buttons stamped with arrows pointing up, down, left and right; on the right, a wooden stand with an artifact taking shape on it; between them a counter that runs to <b>12</b>. Our reads show <b>1/12</b> at 4:10, <b>6/12</b> at 5:20 and <b>7/12</b> at 5:40. At <b>4:41</b> the screen names the thing on the stand: <b>Clay sculpture</b>.
      <br><br>
      We are not printing the twelve moves. At one frame per second the button states change between frames, so a sequence written out here would be our reconstruction, not something the footage shows.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-arrow-panel.jpg" alt="The twelve-step arrow panel in Pharaoh's Tomb, five vertical tracks of round arrow buttons beside a stand holding the clay sculpture, counter showing progress out of twelve" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=250s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:10</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=300s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:00</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=320s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:20</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 5:55 to 6:10 · the case</span>
      <strong>Scarab, collar and a 3×3 keypad</strong>
      The glass display case is the level's showpiece. On the left, a winged <b>blue scarab</b> with a red sun disc above its head; on the right, a segmented <b>broad collar</b> in blue and pale green; between them, a grey <b>3×3 keypad</b> with a dark display screen and backspace/confirm keys either side of the zero. The run types into it here — but the display is small enough that our two crops of it disagree, so this page prints no digits. If you are stuck at the case, this is the one place in the room where the answer is a number, and it is worth re-watching the seconds either side of <b>5:55</b> for the clues that feed it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-display-keypad.jpg" alt="The glass display case in Pharaoh's Tomb with a winged blue scarab, a broad collar and a 3x3 keypad between them" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=355s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:55</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=366s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:06</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 6:35 · the face</span>
      <strong>Four blocks, two eyes and a mouth</strong>
      A low track at the front of the room holds four square wooden blocks, and the puzzle is entirely in their cut-outs: two blocks with vertical slots, one with a horizontal slot above two vertical slots, and a fourth that repeats the first. Slid into the right order they read as <b>a pair of eyes above a mouth</b> — a face assembled from holes rather than drawn on. Nothing is typed here; it is an ordering puzzle, so the shape each block contributes is the clue.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-face-blocks.jpg" alt="The four wooden blocks in Pharaoh's Tomb with rounded cut-outs that form two eyes and a mouth" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=395s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:35</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 6:54 to 7:06 · the door</span>
      <strong>The Anubis head, and a door that stays shut</strong>
      The last pickup is a dark stone <b>Anubis head</b>, jackal-eared with gold detailing around the eyes and collar, sparkle-marked as an item you can take. The camera then returns to the room and the orange door icon lights up over the wooden door between the two standing statues — and the game's own banner appears across the middle of the screen: <b>This is the exit. Locked!</b> The video ends there, at <b>7:06</b>. There is no door animation and no results card, so this page cannot tell you what the door wants, and does not pretend to.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-anubis-head.jpg" alt="The Anubis head pickup in Pharaoh's Tomb, a dark stone jackal-headed bust with gold detailing" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l1-exit-locked.jpg" alt="The exit door in Pharaoh's Tomb with the game banner reading This is the exit. Locked" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=414s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:54</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2SzJM3nlxkg&t=421s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:01</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You expect the tomb to be one long chain.</b> It isn't. Chapter 3's rooms punished you for working out of order; Pharaoh's Tomb does not. If the twelve-step panel is grinding you down, leave it and clear the difference board, the jars and the blocks — nothing locks.</p>
    <p><b>You read the papyrus board as a picture jigsaw.</b> It is the opposite: fifteen tiles, one hole, and repeating hieroglyph symbols. Sliding puzzles like this are solved by getting the <b>top row</b> and <b>left column</b> correct and never disturbing them again — not by nudging the whole board toward a picture.</p>
    <p><b>You hunt for a keypad on the wall.</b> The only keypad in this room is inside the glass display case, next to the scarab. If you are tapping walls looking for digits, you are in the wrong place.</p>
    <p><b>You count the difference-board rings as mistakes.</b> They are progress. The counter runs <b>0/10 → 10/10</b> and each ring marks a pair you have already matched; the frames at 2:10 and 2:15 show the counter climbing as more rings appear.</p>
    <p><b>You finish everything and the door still will not open.</b> That matches the recording — the run ends on <b>This is the exit. Locked!</b> with the Anubis head in hand. The door needs something this clip never shows you, and we would rather say that than invent a step.</p>

    <h2>Where Pharaoh's Tomb sits in Chapter 4<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 4 is <b>Pharaoh's Bride</b>, and it is a museum tour rather than a house: Pharaoh's Tomb, Power and Security, The Office, Planetarium, Dino World, Aeronautics Museum, Art Museum, Illusions and Puzzles, Turkish Exhibition, Botanical Garden, Music Museum, Medical History, Mayas and Incas, Vikings Museum, Japanese Museum. Pharaoh's Tomb is room <b>1 of 15</b>, and it doubles as the chapter's tutorial: it introduces the exhibit-hall layout, the wall-panel puzzle language and the carried-item inventory that the next fourteen rooms reuse.</p>
    <p>Mechanically it is the widest room in the chapter so far — ten interactive stations, four minigames and two carried items — but the shallowest: no puzzle here needs the output of another. That makes it a good room to learn the chapter's grammar in, and a slightly misleading one to judge the rest by.</p>

