    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Three things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are gaps.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The keypad code in the display case.</b> There is a real 3×3 keypad behind the glass, and the run types into it between roughly <b>5:55</b> and <b>6:10</b>. But the display is a few dozen pixels wide in the 1080p frame: one crop came back reading digits, another read nothing at all, and a third read a different set. Two passes that disagree do not make a code, so no digits appear on this page. If you have the code, it belongs here — send it and we will verify it against the frames.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The twelve moves on the arrow panel.</b> The panel is on screen for nearly ninety seconds and the counter clearly climbs to twelve, but at one frame per second the button positions change between frames. The solved state is visible; the path to it is not recoverable from this footage.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The ending, and what the door wants.</b> The file is complete — 426 seconds, decoded to the last frame — and it ends on <b>This is the exit. Locked!</b> with no door animation, no results card and no reward. That is a fact about the video, not a broken download. The Anubis head picked up at 6:54 is never used on camera, so we do not claim it is the door's answer.
    </div>
